-- ================================================================
-- guardians.vocabulary — the trust lattice, and nothing that is not it.
--
-- `Text` is an ordinary sort with an ordinary type parameter. The only unusual
-- thing is WHAT the parameter ranges over — a trust level rather than an element
-- type — and because anthill type arguments unify, that one choice is the whole
-- information-flow mechanism.
--
-- What belongs here is what every other file builds on and none owns. Anything
-- with a subject of its own has left: mail to email.anthill, the triage
-- declarations to spec.anthill.
-- ================================================================

-- Two points is all this example needs; an n-point lattice would cost O(n^2)
-- coercions (docs/design/high-level-api.md D2).
enum guardians.TrustLevel
  entity Untrusted            -- came from outside; may be attacker-authored
  entity Public               -- cleared to leave the system
end

-- THE PARAMETER IS NOT A WORKAROUND. It is the only way an inner label reaches a
-- signature: without it `Text` is one type and `Email.send(body: Text[Public])`
-- cannot be written at all.
--
-- The `= ?` IS one. `sort Trust = ?` admits anything — `Text[Int64]` loads clean,
-- measured — so `TrustLevel` is what these arguments are MEANT to inhabit, not
-- what they are required to. Not a hole (a sink demands a literal label, and
-- `Text[Int64]` reaches nothing); the unsound part is only the claim that the
-- parameter ranges over a lattice. WI-20260822-T70A2, proposal 062.
enum guardians.Text
  import anthill.prelude.{String}
  sort Trust = ?
  entity text(raw: String)
end

-- NO TRANSITIONS BETWEEN LEVELS, and that is a finding. `widen` and `declassify`
-- were declared and never called — `declassify` could not be, since nothing mints
-- its `Approval` token — while `text(raw: u.raw)` re-labels any `Text[Untrusted]`
-- as `Text[Public]` in one line and loads clean. A guarded door beside an open
-- window. The lattice pins a label AT A SINK (rejected/leak.anthill); it does not
-- confine how a label CHANGES. WI-20260829-MCKTE.

-- CAPABILITIES ARE THE SORTS YOU ACQUIRE, so no marker sort stands in for one.
-- `LiveLlm provides Llm` is the sub-capability edge for real, so `-Permission[Llm]`
-- forbids `Permission[X]` for every `X <: Llm` (rejected/frontier_checker.anthill).
--
-- THAT IS ENTAILMENT, NOT THE DECLARED CONTRAVARIANCE, and they run opposite ways.
-- `fact Contravariant(sort: Permission, param: T)` is subsumption; the closure runs
-- covariantly in the capability and needed its own kernel rule
-- (`typing::permission_entails`) — the one thing 064 predicted would come free and
-- did not.

-- A second project effect, so the row checks have a label to widen BY that is not
-- `Modify`. The two widenings travel different arms of one check — a named label
-- as a TYPE, a `Modify` target as a RESOURCE (§5.6) — so wide_row.anthill and
-- wide_row_modify.anthill each catch a program the other does not.
sort guardians.Filesystem
end

namespace guardians
  import anthill.prelude.{Effect}
  import guardians.{Filesystem}

  -- The project's one effect KIND. Capabilities are arguments of `Permission[…]`,
  -- never bare row labels; `Permission` itself the prelude registers.
  fact Effect[T = Filesystem]
end
